<!DOCTYPE DM SYSTEM>
<!-- Help/tutorial panel for the ISPF Primary Option Menu. Shown on PF1. The
     tutorial is longer than one 24-row screen, so the server pages it with
     PF7/PF8 (server._show_help, #281); PF3 returns. Auto-flow (ISPDTLC-idiomatic):
     no row/col; the title centres itself and the conversion utility spaces the
     blocks. The option grid is a preformatted <lines> block so its columns line
     up. -->
<panel name="ispfhelp">ISPF Primary Option Menu - HELP
  <info>
    <p>The Primary Option Menu is the top of the ISPF dialog hierarchy. From it
       you reach every ISPF function by selecting a numbered option.</p>

    <p>Type an option number on the Option ===> line and press ENTER to select it.</p>
    <lines>
0  Settings      1  View          2  Edit          3  Utilities
4  Foreground    5  Batch         6  Command       7  Dialog Test
9  IBM Products  10 SCLM          11 Workplace     12 z/OS System
</lines>

    <p>Enter X (or press PF3) on the Option line to exit ISPF and log off.</p>

    <p>Option 0 (Settings) tailors your ISPF session: the command line placement,
       the terminal characteristics, the list and log data set dispositions, and
       the function-key definitions.</p>

    <p>Options 1 and 2 (View and Edit) open a member of the panel library. View
       shows the source read-only; Edit opens it for change. Type a member name on
       the entry panel and press ENTER.</p>

    <p>Option 3 (Utilities) groups the data-management functions: the Library
       utility (3.1) lists the members of a library so you can browse, rename, or
       delete them; other utilities allocate, copy, and print data sets.</p>

    <p>Option 6 (Command) is a TSO command shell. Option 7 (Dialog Test) exercises
       the dialog manager, including the variable display and the table-input test.</p>

    <p>Long panels like this tutorial scroll: press PF8 (or PF11) to page forward
       and PF7 (or PF10) to page back. The "More: - +" indicator on the bottom
       line shows whether more text lies above (-) or below (+).</p>

    <p>Press PF3 at any time to leave HELP and return to the Primary Option Menu.</p>
  </info>

  <botinst>PF7=Up  PF8=Down  PF3=Return</botinst>

  <keyl name="HELPKEYS">
    <keyi key="PF3"  cmd="EXIT">Return</keyi>
    <keyi key="PF15" cmd="EXIT">Return</keyi>
  </keyl>
</panel>
